; StyleBoxFlat's two WHOLE-PASS draw stages — `skew` and the drop shadow — on
; four Panels of identical size over a light backdrop.
;
; The one variable: the stages `StyleBoxFlat::draw` runs, rather than the
; parameters of the ring it already draws. Every other stylebox scene varies
; the ring itself (radii, border widths, blend); neither field here changes a
; radius or a width, so a regression in either is invisible in all of them.
; Same node type, same 480x260 rect, same grid pitch, same fill colour
; throughout — only which stages run changes.
;
; SkewSharp is the one that cannot be faked. Its corners are all sharp and its
; skew is horizontal only, so a builder that ignored `skew` draws a plain
; axis-aligned rectangle and the difference is the whole silhouette. It also
; pins the second, easily-missed half of the field: `aa_on` is
; `(rounded_corners || !skew.is_zero_approx()) && anti_aliased`, so a skewed
; box antialiases even with sharp corners, and its diagonal edges are the only
; place in the goldens where that term is exercised. A port that read only the
; radii would leave those edges hard-stepped.
;
; SkewRound carries a skew on BOTH axes plus a border and one radius, which is
; what proves the shear is applied to every ring about ONE centre: shear each
; ring about its own centre instead and the border detaches from the fill,
; visibly thickening on two sides and thinning on the other two.
;
; ShadowOffset is a shadow displaced down-right with no border, so the shadow
; and the box are separable by eye: the box's own edges stay where an
; unshadowed box would put them, and everything outside them is the new stage.
; `shadow_size` is large (24) against a light backdrop because the shadow is a
; fade to alpha 0 — a small one is indistinguishable from an antialiasing
; feather, which is exactly the confusion a golden should not have to arbitrate.
;
; ShadowHollow pairs the shadow with `draw_center = false`. Godot fills the
; shadow's interior only when the box draws its own centre, so this pane's
; shadow is a ring too and the backdrop shows through both — the case that
; separates "draw the shadow" from "draw a shadow-coloured rectangle behind
; the box". It also offsets the shadow UP-LEFT, the opposite of ShadowOffset,
; so a sign error in `shadow_offset` cannot pass both.
;
; The backdrop is deliberately much lighter than every box, for the same reason
; the sibling stylebox scene gives: a fade to transparent and a diagonal edge
; are only measurable against something they contrast with.
;
; Measured against Godot 4.6.3 at `--mode 2d`: whole-frame mean channel error
; 0.57, with 29 pixels past 40 counts and none past 90. Both skews land on the
; engine's own silhouette, and the shadow's fade agrees to within a count
; across its whole gradient — a transect through the pure-shadow band right of
; ShadowOffset reads rgb(83, 74, 116) against Godot's rgb(84, 75, 116) and
; tracks it step for step out to the backdrop. What is left is one-pixel
; silhouette coverage on the shadow's outer arc and on the skewed panes'
; diagonals, where this renderer's multisample resolve and Godot's
; un-antialiased rasterizer disagree about a partly-covered pixel; the same
; residual is visible on every diagonal edge in the golden set and is not
; specific to either field here.

[gd_scene load_steps=5 format=3]

[sub_resource type="StyleBoxFlat" id="StyleBoxFlat_skew_sharp"]
bg_color = Color(0.15, 0.3, 0.5, 1)
skew = Vector2(0.35, 0)

[sub_resource type="StyleBoxFlat" id="StyleBoxFlat_skew_round"]
